@@ -3,6 +3,72 @@
 All notable changes to `rightkit-shell`. Versions follow Cargo semver; while the
 version is `0.x`, a minor bump may carry breaking changes.
 
+## 0.4.0
+
+Round-2 gap pass (Orthic-Labs/rightkit#386, #670, #481, #486, #498, #672, #746, #414, #640, #638, #232). Not built or run locally: the main chat compiles and tests. Minor bump because public types gain variants and fields (see "Breaking" below).
+
+### Breaking (0.x minor)
+
+- `MenuEntry` gains the variants `Check` and `Submenu`: an exhaustive `match` on it needs two arms. `MenuSpec` gains fields (`file`, `edit_extra`, `view_entries`, `view_fullscreen`, `window_entries`, `window_compact`, `app_compact`, `omit_empty_help`, `overrides`); it derives `Default`, so use `..Default::default()`.
+- `windows::WindowSpec` gains `parent`, `visible`, `resizable`, `shadow`; `ShellState` gains `menu_checks`, `open_paths`, `surfaces`, `host`, `pinned_origin`, `destroy_on_close`, `on_recreate`, `drop_events`; `CloseBehavior` gains `DestroyAndRecreate`; `engine::EnginePolicy` gains `stream`. A struct literal of any of these without `..Default::default()` needs updating.
+- `menu::build` refuses an empty or duplicate item id (`menu::validate`), so a runtime update by id is unambiguous.
+- With `--all-features`, the `wv2-0_38` types win over `wv2-0_39` (the workspace lock resolves Tauri 2.11), where `wv2-0_39` used to win.
+
+### Native menu (#386)
+
+- `MenuEntry::Check { id, label, accelerator, enabled, checked }` and `MenuEntry::Submenu { label, entries }` (JSON `kind: "check"` / `"submenu"`; helpers `MenuEntry::check`, `MenuEntry::submenu`). Submenus nest up to `MAX_SUBMENU_DEPTH` (4). `menu::entry_ids` lists ids. The tray still takes only `Item` and `Separator`; a `Check` or `Submenu` there makes `tray::install`, `TrayHandle` and `rk_shell_set_tray` return an error.
+- Runtime state by id: `rk_shell_set_menu_item_checked(id, checked)` and the batch `rk_shell_set_menu_state(updates: [{id, enabled?, checked?}])` (`menu::MenuItemState`, `menu::apply_state`, `menu::check_state`). `rk_shell_set_menu_item_enabled` also finds check items. In a batch every valid update applies and the refusals come back in one error. A native click toggles a check item's tick itself; the app corrects it with `setChecked` if it disagrees. Clicks on every item id, custom ones included, arrive as `rightkit-shell://menu` `{ id }`.
+- Menu layout (`menu::plan`, pure): HIG order App (macOS), File, Edit, View, app groups, Window, Help. `MenuSpec.file` adds or extends File; `edit_extra` appends entries (a Find submenu, say) to Edit; `view_entries` / `view_fullscreen` shape View; `window_entries` / `window_compact` shape Window; `omit_empty_help` skips an empty Help; `app_compact` drops About and Services from the macOS app menu; `overrides: [MenuOverride { role: Undo | Redo | Quit | CloseWindow, id, label?, accelerator? }]` swaps a native item for an app-id item that keeps the native label and accelerator and emits `rightkit-shell://menu`. Defaults build exactly the earlier layout. The app-menu quit item stays the shell's own item (`ID_QUIT`, routed to the ordered quit), so close-to-tray still cannot swallow Cmd+Q.
+
+### Windows and WebView2 (#386, #498)
+
+- `windows::WindowSpec` gains `parent` (label of an existing window; an unknown parent is an error), `visible` (`false` creates the window hidden), `resizable` and `shadow`.
+- `rk_shell_window` takes optional `width`, `height` and `value` and the actions `resize` (logical inner size, 1..=16384), `center`, `set-resizable`, `set-maximizable`, `set-fullscreen` (explicit `value`; `fullscreen` still only toggles) and `destroy` (no close-requested round trip). Parsing is `windows::parse_window_op` / `WindowOp`.
+- `webview2_menu` (custom `ContextMenuRequested` items per frame kind, `disable_browser_accelerator_keys`, `route_new_windows`), `print` (`ShowPrintUI`), `preview` (token-keyed in-memory preview scheme with a per-kind CSP), `webview2_env` (background colour and `WEBVIEW2_USER_DATA_FOLDER`, set before the first webview) and `os_settings` (`ms-settings:` pages). Windows only where they touch COM; their decisions are pure and unit-tested.
+- #498: `window_icon::apply_icon` / `apply_default_icon` send `WM_SETICON` (32 px big, 16 px small, area-averaged from RGBA) so the taskbar and Alt+Tab show the app icon; `ShellBuilder::sync_window_icon(true)` does it for every webview as it becomes ready (Windows only; a no-op elsewhere). `startup_error::show` / `show_and_exit` put a fatal start-up error in a native dialog (`MessageBoxW`, `osascript`, `zenity` / `kdialog`) and stderr; no dialog in a `RIGHTKIT_QA_HIDDEN=1` run. The WebView2 user-data folder is `webview2_env`.
+- `windows::open_window(app, &spec)` is `open` returning the `WebviewWindow` (so a caller can attach state or handlers); `open` keeps returning the label. Not done: a runtime-generic `open` (CodeRight's mini chat, canvas and bubble windows build with `WebviewWindowBuilder` themselves): the shell's builder, frameless style and reveal guard are `Wry`-only, so generalising them is its own change.
+- New `open_paths` module and `rk_shell_open_paths_ready`: file paths from cold-start argv and every forwarded second launch are filtered (`is_path_arg`: no flags, URLs or control characters; relative paths join the second launch's cwd), queued (at most 256) until the renderer is ready, then emitted as `rightkit-shell://open-paths` `{ paths }`. Paths are not granted: use the broker.
+
+### DevTools (settled decision rightkit#771 D2)
+
+- `devtools::set_inspector_shortcut_scope(app, label, enabled)` scopes the native inspector keys (macOS `developerExtrasEnabled`, Windows F12 / Ctrl+Shift+I/J/C / Ctrl+U) to one webview at run time. The keys are OFF by default and the call is Rust-only (no JS command). Enabling is refused unless the build compiled DevTools in (`content-devtools`, which only ViewRight may ship, or the dev-only `dev-inspect`) and the policy does not deny that webview; disabling always works. macOS also keeps the WebKit element picker off for the life of an opened inspector.
+
+### File associations (#670)
+
+- New `associations` module, generalised from ViewRight `mac_associations`. The app passes `AssociationSpec { bundle_id, groups: [AssociationGroup { id, extensions, utis, windows_prog_id }] }` per call. `associations::status` reports the default handler per group and target (macOS: UTIs through NSWorkspace then `LSCopyDefaultRoleHandlerForContentType`; Windows: `extension:.ext` from HKCU `UserChoice\ProgId`, then HKCU / HKCR class default, ProgID compared case-insensitively). `associations::set_defaults` makes the running app the default on macOS (NSWorkspace set on the main thread, one request at a time, one shared timeout 1..=120 s default 60 s, the legacy call only when the selector is absent, result re-read, refused unless the running bundle id equals the spec's) and on Windows opens Default apps and reports `user_action_required` (`UserChoice` is never written). `associations::open_default_apps` uses `os_settings::DEFAULT_APPS`. Commands `rk_shell_associations_status`, `rk_shell_associations_set`, `rk_shell_open_default_apps`. The `windows` dependency gains `Win32_System_Registry`.
+
+### Engine and tray (#481, #486, #746)
+
+- `rk_shell_engine_subscribe(onEvent: Channel, snapshotMethod?, params?, tag?)`: a snapshot-atomic stream. The receiver attaches first, then the allowlisted snapshot method runs, so no event between the snapshot and the subscription is lost; the stream carries the same frames as `rightkit-shell://engine` plus `$lifecycle` changes (and `$unsolicited` frames), the snapshot call's own events stay off it, and a later subscription replaces an earlier one. `EnginePolicy::stream_coalesce(StreamCoalesce)` coalesces progress events by key (`engine_stream`: `EventHub`, `StreamCoalesce`, `Coalescer`, `run_forwarder`; the `rightkit-shell://engine` event is never coalesced).
+- The allowlist, per-method deadlines, request tags, restart policy (`EnginePolicy`) and the custom-only, multi-id tray (`TrayOptions`) shipped in 0.3.2; this release adds their renderer half (`@rightkit/tauri-shell` 0.2.0: `engine.callTagged`, `engine.status`, `engine.subscribe`, `trayId`).
+
+### Surfaces, dialogs, drag-drop (#746)
+
+- `ShellBuilder::surface(name, width, height)` and `rk_shell_set_surface(surface)`: the page names the surface it is about to show; Rust sizes (restoring a maximised window first), centres and shows the window through the reveal guard, so nothing resizes after it appears. Pure registry and tolerance rule in `surface`.
+- `dialog` (new feature `dialog`, optional `tauri-plugin-dialog`; the app also registers `tauri_plugin_dialog::init()` and its capability): `rk_shell_pick_files`, `rk_shell_pick_folder`, `rk_shell_save_dialog`. Picked files and save targets are granted in the path broker before the page sees them. Without the feature the commands answer `unsupported`. Adding the optional dependency needs `Cargo.lock` to be regenerated once (`cargo update -p rightkit-tauri-shell`).
+- `ShellBuilder::drop_events(true)`: native drag-drop phases as `rightkit-shell://drop` `{ phase, label, x, y, paths?, rejected? }` with dropped files granted first (`dragdrop`).
+
+### In-process settings, models and secrets (#672)
+
+- New `host` module and `ShellBuilder::host_handlers(HostHandlers)`: the nine `@rightkit/settings-ui` shell-adapter methods (`rightkit.settings.get|patch`, `rightkit.models.list|install|cancel|remove`, `rightkit.secrets.presence|set|clear`) and the `rightkit.settings.changed` / `rightkit.models.entry` events are answered in the app's process, before any engine sidecar, so an app with no sidecar gets working preferences, models and credential management. Three traits: `SettingsHost`, `ModelsHost`, `SecretsHost`; a group left unregistered goes on to the engine as before. Errors reach the page as `"<code>: <message>"` (legacy call) or `{code, message, origin: "host"}` (tagged); a stale `expectedRevision` is `revision_conflict`.
+- Ready adapters behind optional features: `host-app-settings` (`AppSettingsHost` over a `rightkit-app-settings` `Store<Map<String, Value>>`, compare-and-set through `update_at`, change events from the store's subscription) and `host-credentials` (`CredentialsHost` over a `rightkit-credentials` `Store`, including `clear`, which deletes the entry; a ref outside the store's namespace is `unknown` / refused).
+
+### Close, recreate, pickers, consent (#414, #640, #638)
+
+- `CloseBehavior::DestroyAndRecreate` and `ShellBuilder::on_recreate(f)`: closing the main window destroys it (webview freed) while the process stays up; when the shell would reveal the main window (tray click, second launch, Dock reopen, opened URL, `windows::reveal`) and finds none, it calls `f(app, reveal_source)` to build it again. A never-show run recreates nothing. Rule: `lifecycle::close_action`.
+- `pickers::pick_folder` / `pick_app`: plain-Rust native pickers (`osascript`, PowerShell with the data passed in environment variables, `zenity` / `kdialog`), no Tauri window; `Ok(None)` is a cancel. The non-Tauri single instance already shipped as `SingleInstance::claim`.
+- `consent`: unspoofable native consent (Deny / Allow once / Always allow) for an external protocol or a host enrollment, with per-origin remembered grants. `consent_surface(pinned, current)` decides whether a grant may be used or created (only when the visible top-level origin equals the pinned origin; a foreign page gets Allow-once only and no "Always allow"); `GrantStore` is the app's persistence; `grants_in` / `add_grant` are the JSON helpers (malformed grants read as no grant, duplicates are not added); `decide` is the policy over an `ask` closure and `confirm` uses the native dialog. Fail closed: no dialog, a hidden QA run or any error is `Deny`, and a store that cannot save degrades "Always allow" to "Allow once".
+- `ShellBuilder::pinned_origin(origin)`: the popup hook now passes the pinned origin to `NavPolicy::decide_window_open_pinned`, so a popup opens in-app only when its opener is exactly on that origin (closes the "partial" noted for #638 in 0.3.2). `WindowOpenDecision` is unchanged: `NavPolicy::protocol_consent_scheme` stays the way an app learns a non-web `window.open` target needs consent.
+
+### Tauri-free core (#519)
+
+- `tauri` is now an optional dependency behind the default feature `tauri`. With `default-features = false` (plus `wv2-0_38` or `wv2-0_39` on Windows, which supplies the `windows` crate the clipboard uses) the crate has no `tauri` dependency and still provides `ShellError`, `PathBroker`, the rich clipboard (`clipboard`, `ClipboardItem`), `autostart` and `login_item`, `SingleInstance` and the legacy-name helpers, `notify`, `presence`, `recovery`, `startup_cache`, `quit`, `consent`, `pickers`, `startup_error`, `dragdrop`, `surface`, `engine_stream` and the `host` handler traits. Every module that needs Tauri (plugin, builder, commands, menu, tray, windows, nav, engine, ...) is behind the feature; their code is in `src/shell.rs`, included from `lib.rs` so crate-root paths do not change. `engine` and `dialog` imply `tauri`. Default builds are unchanged.
+- Not compiled without Tauri: `host::app_sink` (the Tauri event sink); tests and the `budget` bench of the shell are gated on `tauri`.
+
+### Tests
+
+New unit tests in `host`, `consent`, `pickers`, `startup_error`, `window_icon`, `surface`, `dialog`, `dragdrop`, `engine_stream`, `lifecycle::close_action`, `menu`, `associations`, `open_paths`; integration tests `tests/menu_entries.rs`, `tests/menu_layout.rs`, `tests/open_paths.rs`, `tests/webview2_helpers.rs`, `tests/associations.rs`, additions to `tests/window_spec.rs`. COM, AppKit, registry and dialog calls need a run on Windows and on a Mac. `runtime-budget.json` evidence still names 0.3.2; re-measure or re-pin it if the budget guard compares versions.
+
